{"ts":"2026-07-26T22:19:28.444Z","name":"gampleman/elm-visualization","version":"2.4.3","commit":"4527b53","dirty":true,"status":"working-failure","reason":"exit-1","platform":"pure","ms":343965,"signature":"unclassified:no-evidence","evidence":"[phase] extract ryan-haskell/date-format\n[phase] transform ryan-haskell/date-format\n[phase] extract gampleman/elm-visualization\ngampleman/elm-visualization@2.4.3 (extract cached)\n[phase] transform gampleman/elm-visualization\nAMBIGUOUS_MODULE_NAME: Package gampleman/elm-visualization exposes the module `Force`, but its dependency ianmackenzie/elm-units (as ianmackenzie/elm-units) already exposes that name. Elm allows two packages to share a module name when no single module imports both; Gren forbids the collision at the package graph (even when no module imports both). This is a language-level difference, not a porting bug. Automatic rename is refused: it would change a public API, is not a pure function of the module name alone (both packages use the same name, so D72 does not apply), and a graph-dependent rename would make the same package emit different Gren under different roots. The `add` command's Elm. module prefix does not help (both sides become Elm.Force and still collide). What you can do: fork one of the two Elm packages and rename the colliding module there (and its importers) so the exposed names no longer clash, then port the fork; or drop a dependency only when the edge is false (see D76). There is no namespacing flag that resolves a same-name collision between two vendored packages."}
